---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import { BRAND_NAME } from '~/lib/site';
import badge from '~/assets/logo/logo-badge.svg';
---

<Base
  title={`How We Verify Dog Policies | ${BRAND_NAME}`}
  description="Every dog policy on this site is checked against an official source, dated, and re-checked every 90 days. Here's exactly how."
>
  <div class="wrap prose page">
    <div class="verify-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Trust</p>
        <h1>How we verify</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Dog policies are the least stable facts in travel. Patio rules change
          with managers, beach access changes with seasons, and county ordinances
          change with board votes. Most directories publish once and never look
          back. We built this site around not doing that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="field-badge"
        src={badge.src}
        alt="Dog-Eared Guides — field-tested"
        width="150"
        height="150"
      />
    </div>

    <h2>Every fact needs a source</h2>
    <p>
      No dog-policy claim is published here without a recorded source — the
      venue's official website or social page, the managing agency's rules
      page, the county code, or a direct phone confirmation. If we can't
      verify a claim officially, we either leave it out or label it an open
      question. We never copy policies from other directories.
    </p>

    <h2>The "Verified" badge</h2>
    <p>
      Every venue page shows a <strong>Verified</strong> date: the last day a
      human-reviewed check confirmed the dog policy against its source. We also
      record <em>how</em> it was verified — official website, phone call, or
      in person — and link the source where one exists.
    </p>

    <h2>Two badges, three tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Not every dog-friendly place says so in writing. Some venues welcome
      dogs every day and never mention it on their website. Our listings
      distinguish the difference instead of papering over it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Verified (green)</strong> — the policy is confirmed by an
        official source: the venue's own site or the managing agency's rules.
        The source is linked on the page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visitor-reported (amber)</strong> — the venue publishes no
        policy, but at least two independent visitor accounts describe dogs
        being welcomed. The page links every report we relied on, says
        plainly that the venue hasn't confirmed it, and claims nothing the
        reports don't support.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Neither</strong> — no official source and no usable visitor
        evidence. Those venues simply don't appear here until evidence does.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Visitor-reported listings are candidates for upgrade: when a venue
      publishes a policy or confirms one by phone, the listing moves to the
      green badge with the new source attached.
    </p>

    <h2>The 90-day rule</h2>
    <p>
      Any listing whose verification is older than 90 days is automatically
      queued for re-checking. If a policy changed, the page changes. If a
      venue closed, the listing comes down. This runs on a schedule — it
      doesn't depend on anyone remembering.
    </p>

    <h2>Seasonal rules are first-class</h2>
    <p>
      In mountain and lake towns, "dog-friendly" often means "dog-friendly
      from May to October." We record seasonal restrictions — winter trail
      closures, summer-only patios, ski-season Nordic trail bans — as part of
      the policy itself, not as a footnote.
    </p>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p>
      If a listing doesn't match what you found on the ground, we want to know.
      Flag it and we'll re-verify. A guide like this is only worth
      anything if it's right.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 2rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .verify-hero {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 2rem;
  }
  .field-badge {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    transform: rotate(6deg);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .field-badge {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
